  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "TvRWFGN7N9V6WfDQWRiIWn7hzMk=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:24:28.542Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The artefact proposed for inovex is not a client integration but a reference architecture: Switchboard deployed the Kubernetes-native way, packaged so the firm's consultants can lift it into successive engagements. The unit of value is reuse across projects, which matches how a consultancy of that size actually absorbs new technology.\n\nThe research attaches an explicit caution to it. At roughly 500 people the decision is a committee rather than a champion, so this is a partnership conversation and not an outbound one. A reference architecture is the right object to bring to that conversation precisely because it is something the partner can own and resell, rather than a favour asked of one engineer.\n\nThat also changes the success criterion relative to the small-partner demos elsewhere in the corpus. This one succeeds when inovex's delivery organisation adopts it as a standard \u2014 a slower and more political outcome than shipping a single adapter, and one that should be planned for as such.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- inovex GmbH (DE Karlsruhe +6 + CH Bern) \u2014 Stephan M\u00fcller + Tobias Joch, Gesch\u00e4ftsf\u00fchrer \u00b7 info@inovex.de \u00b7 https://inovex.de\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Kubernetes-native Switchboard reference architecture their consultants can reuse per engagement. NOTE: 500-person firm \u2014 committee sale, treat as a partnership motion, not outbound.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:24:28.542876Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:24:28.542880Z"
    }
  },
